Title: SCSS Ontology & Guardrails

Purpose: document the current semantic stack, describe the future scaffolds from `design/scss_ontology_scaffolding.md`, and give agents clear rules for extending the palette cleanly.

Sections:

1. Semantic Token Map
   - All colors originate from `src/scss/base/_variables.scss`, which will become a map (`$brand-colors`) so every semantic slot (primary, highlight, support, background, etc.) is defined once.
   - Add new entries to the map, expose them via `@function brand-color($key)` and, eventually, through CSS variables (`--brand-<key>`). Never sprinkle raw hex values outside of `_variables.scss`.
   - `$highlight-color` / `brand-color('saffron')` (#c88304) stays the main page hero tone; new accents should harmonize with that direction and the complementary trio (gold/coral/cerulean).

2. CSS Custom Property Layer
   - Build a generator that emits `:root` custom properties from the token map (and writes theme-specific overrides inside `.dark-mode`, `.light-mode`).
   - Components can then use `var(--brand-highlight)` interchangeably with Sass helpers, which makes runtime theming and HeyGen exports easier.
   - Keep the map → CSS var pipeline in `_variables.scss` so the token hub remains the single source of truth.

3. Theme Modules & `@use` Discipline
   - Convert `_variables.scss`, `_themes/_dark.scss`, `_mixins.scss`, and `_functions.scss` into `@use`-able modules (`@use 'base/variables' as vars;`).
   - Theme files should only redefine semantic slots and complementary helpers; they must not re-implement component logic.
    - Prefer `@use` for all local SCSS modules and reserve `@import` only for remote resources (fonts/CDNs) so the compiler stays compatible with Dart Sass 3.0+.
   - Document the module graph (see `design/scss_ontology_scaffolding.md`) so future contributors know which module exports what.

4. Guardrail Automation
   - `pnpm run validate:brand` executes `scripts/validate-brand-colors.js`, which enforces that only the colors declared in `$brand-colors` appear as raw hex values outside the approved skip list.
   - Add the script to your watch/test pipeline (`pnpm test` already runs it) so the brand-color contract is enforced before shipping new SCSS changes.
    - When manipulating colors (darken/lighten/grayscale/scale), `@use 'sass:color' as color` and call the namespaced helpers (`color.scale`, `color.grayscale`, etc.) rather than the deprecated global functions so Dart Sass 3.0+ never throws a warning.
   - Use `@use 'sass:map' as map` whenever a Sass map lookup is needed (`map.get`), keeping global `map-get` calls out of the code.

4. Utility Functions
   - `_functions.scss` remains the home for contrast/luminance helpers; any new ratio-based logic belongs there before being used elsewhere.
   - If a new helper (e.g., `brand-contrast()`) is needed to support the token map, add it here so contrast decisions stay consistent.

5. Mixins & Shared Layouts
   - `_mixins.scss` continues to define `%button-base`, `%card-base`, `@include flex-center`, media mixins, animations, and now includes spacing scale mixins plus brand gradient/shadow/text helpers that call `brand-color()`.
   - The mixin file also exposes responsive container, brand border, and brand transition helpers alongside the `@layer utilities` block so agents can reuse container/layout helpers without copying new selectors.
   - We surface an `@layer utilities` block with spacing, text, background, gradient, shadow, responsive container, border, and transition classes so agents can reuse consistent helpers instead of inventing new utility selectors.
   - New UI pieces should extend these shared mixins instead of recreating repeatable flex/spacing logic.
   - Keep spacing derived from the core breakpoints/section height tokens so layouts stay aligned with the brand rhythm.

6. Reset & Base Styles
   - `_reset.scss` still enforces box-sizing, `body` defaults, and the base background/font from the token hub.
   - Never override these resets at the component level; let theme wrappers adjust them via the semantic map.

7. Guardrails & Review Checklist
   - Before adding a pattern, read `design/scss_ontology_scaffolding.md` to identify which scaffold the work belongs to and which tokens/mixins to reuse.
   - Checklist for new SCSS:
       * Are colors pulled from `$brand-colors` / `brand-color()` rather than hardcoded?
       * Do theme files only redefine semantic values (never component selectors)?
       * Are shared mixins/resets respected (no rogue margin/padding or new global selectors)?
       * Are contrast decisions backed by `_functions.scss` helpers?
       * Are brand gradients/shadows/text helpers used when applying highlight or accent values, and do new utilities live under the `@layer utilities` block so the map remains the single source of truth?
       * Do comments/naming explain whether the token is part of the main saffron story or a channel-specific accent?
   - Update this file whenever a new scaffold (A–D) adds tokens, mixins, or automation so it stays the single source of truth.

Use this rule file alongside `design/scss_ontology_scaffolding.md` to keep all future changes aligned with the curated palette and mixin layers.
